---
title: "Company levy 2026: €409 or €1,022 due by 31 December"
date: 2026-11-09
lang: en
category: Tax
excerpt: "Payment notices issued in November: plan your payment based on your 2024 balance sheet before 31 December."
published: true
author: AgiFid
---

Your company will receive its payment notice in November for the annual company levy. It must be paid by 31 December. The amount depends on a single criterion: the total of your 2024 financial year balance sheet.

## Two amounts in 2026

For the 2026 levy, the calculation is based on the 2024 balance sheet total:

- €409.30 if your 2024 balance sheet is less than €879,163.44

- €1,022.38 if your 2024 balance sheet exceeds €879,163.44

The public authorities use data from the National Bank of Belgium to determine the levy. No need to calculate: the amount will appear on the notice you receive this month.

## Who is affected

Companies subject to Belgian corporate tax or Belgian non-resident tax are required, with few exceptions, to pay this annual levy. This levy contributes to financing the social security system for the self-employed.

Start-up companies (with no balance sheet available for 2024) pay the reduced levy of €409.30.

## Deadline and exceptions

The amount must be in the account of the social insurance fund by 31 December. Late postal submissions are not accepted: it is the date of receipt that counts.

Exception: for companies created from 1 October onwards, the levy must be paid no later than the last day of the third month following the month in which the company was created. Example: company created in November 2026, levy due by 28 February 2027.

## What might change

The Council of Ministers has approved a reform aimed at introducing a more progressive system by replacing the two current amounts with four distinct levy levels. This reform is still subject to final adoption of the bill by Parliament.

For 2026, the applicable amounts remain €409.30 and €1,022.38.

## In practice

Check the notice received in November. If your cash flow is tight at year-end, bring forward payment now. The levy is due regardless of your profitability: even a loss-making company pays this flat-rate contribution.

In Odoo, record this charge in account 618 (social contributions). Payment must be made to your social insurance fund, not to the FPS Finance.
